  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nCrossing surface high pressure provides dry and quiet weather\nthrough tonight. Light wind and relatively low humidity will\nlead to continued pleasant conditions under a mostly sunny sky,\nwith temperatures just a few degrees below climatology. High\nclouds begin to increase tonight on west/southwest flow aloft,\nkeeping low temperatures close to normal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA shortwave trough swings across the Great Lakes on Thursday and\ninto the northeast CONUS by Friday morning. Associated surface\nlow pressure crosses southern Canada, pushing a surface boundary\ntowards the region by the end of the week. There continue to be\nsigns that the boundary may stall somewhere in the Ohio Valley\ninto the weekend, as it aligns with quasi-zonal mid-level flow.\nThis will keep precipitation chances in the forecast each day\nfrom Thursday through at least Saturday, with coverage favoring\nareas south of Pittsburgh from Friday on.\n\nThe initial push of showers and storms Thursday afternoon and\nevening will have the support of the approaching/crossing front\nand trough axis and perhaps a prefrontal surface trough. Storms\nshould fire in a favorable environment, with HREF means \nsuggesting around 1000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE and 40-45 \nknots of 0-6km shear. Severe weather seems a possibility, with \ndamaging wind gusts the likely main threat.\n\nGiven the possibility of repeated rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms, rainfall totals mainly from Pittsburgh on south\nwill need to be monitored into Saturday. Still, precipitable\nwater values in this region generally peak in the 85th to 90th\npercentile - not excessive, but enough to fuel heavy rain totals\nin training situations, which could occur with a quasi-\nstationary boundary in the area for a time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nThe boundary may finally lift back north as a warm front on\nSunday. After this, there is broad ensemble agreement on an\nimpressive 500mb ridge building across the lower Mississippi and\nOhio River Valleys, which would spread to encompass our region\nearly next week. There is a high probability of 500mb heights\nrising above the 500dm level by Tuesday next week. This would\nlikely suppress anything more than isolated afternoon convection\nearly next week. The main impact in this pattern would be the\nincreasingly hot and humid conditions which could develop, with\ntemperatures in the 90s looking increasingly likely to end the\nmonth of June.",
